<http://data.silknow.org/object/f4e656b3-5894-3769-8a8e-3d070b921d3b/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Most surviving handkerchiefs from the 1600s are very decorative and obviously only meant for show. This is a rare example of one meant for practical use.  A linen kerchief was an essential part of the seventeenth century wardrobe.  Women wore them for modesty, to fill in a low-necked bodice or waistcoat.  \n\nIn the days before disposable paper tissues, a linen handkerchief was an essential accessory for men and women.   This example measures approximately 69 cm x 67 cm and could have been used for either purpose.  \n\nVery few historical garments of plain linen survive. When no longer wearable, anything made of linen was sold to a rag merchant and recycled into writing paper."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/f4e656b3-5894-3769-8a8e-3d070b921d3b> .

